# SecurityScorecard OAuth Configuration
# Task: T-015 OAuth Flow for SecurityScorecard
#
# Copy this section to your .env file and fill in the values

# OAuth Client Configuration
# Get these values from SecurityScorecard developer portal
SS_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=your_client_id_here
SS_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=your_client_secret_here

# OAuth Redirect URI
# Must match the redirect URI registered in SecurityScorecard developer portal
# Format: https://your-api-domain.com/sso/securityscorecard/callback
SS_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://api.cyberrx.com/sso/securityscorecard/callback

# OAuth Endpoints (Optional - defaults shown)
# Override these only if SecurityScorecard provides custom endpoints
SS_OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_URL=https://www.securityscorecard.com/oauth/authorize
SS_OAUTH_TOKEN_URL=https://api.securityscorecard.com/oauth/token

# Frontend URL (for OAuth callback redirects)
# Where users are redirected after OAuth completion
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:5173

# =============================================================================
# Setup Instructions
# =============================================================================

# 1. Register OAuth Application
#    - Go to SecurityScorecard Developer Portal
#    - Create a new OAuth 2.0 application
#    - Set redirect URI to: https://your-api-domain.com/sso/securityscorecard/callback
#    - Select scopes: api:read, api:write
#    - Save and copy client ID and secret

# 2. Configure Environment Variables
#    - Add the above variables to your .env file
#    - Replace placeholder values with actual credentials
#    - Ensure redirect URI matches SecurityScorecard portal

# 3. Update Frontend URL
#    - Set FRONTEND_URL to your frontend domain
#    - This is where users are redirected after OAuth

# 4. Test OAuth Flow
#    - Start the API server
#    - Call GET /api/sso/securityscorecard/authorize?organizationId=org-123
#    - Navigate to the returned authorizationUrl
#    - Complete authorization in SecurityScorecard
#    - Verify callback redirects to frontend with success=true

# =============================================================================
# Security Notes
# =============================================================================

# - Never commit .env file with real credentials to version control
# - Use different client secrets for development and production
# - Rotate client secrets periodically
# - Monitor OAuth token usage and audit logs
# - Implement rate limiting on OAuth endpoints
# - Use HTTPS in production for all OAuth redirects

# =============================================================================
# Troubleshooting
# =============================================================================

# Problem: "Missing required OAuth configuration"
# Solution: Ensure all SS_OAUTH_* variables are set in .env

# Problem: "Invalid state parameter"
# Solution: State expired (10 minutes) or CSRF attack suspected

# Problem: "Refresh token expired or revoked"
# Solution: User must re-authenticate via OAuth flow

# Problem: "Connection test failed"
# Solution: Verify OAuth credentials and network connectivity

# =============================================================================
# OAuth Flow Summary
# =============================================================================

# 1. Frontend calls GET /sso/securityscorecard/authorize
#    -> Returns authorization URL with PKCE code challenge

# 2. User navigates to SecurityScorecard authorization page
#    -> User grants permissions

# 3. SecurityScorecard redirects to callback URL
#    -> GET /sso/securityscorecard/callback?code=xxx&state=yyy

# 4. Backend exchanges code for tokens
#    -> POST to token endpoint with code and PKCE verifier
#    -> Stores access_token and refresh_token in vault

# 5. Frontend receives success redirect
#    -> OAuth flow complete, tokens ready for API calls

# 6. Automatic token refresh
#    -> Tokens refresh automatically 5 minutes before expiry
#    -> Uses refresh_token to get new access_token

# 7. Token revocation (optional)
#    -> DELETE /sso/securityscorecard/revoke
#    -> Removes tokens from vault and revokes with provider

# =============================================================================
# API Key vs OAuth
# =============================================================================

# OAuth is recommended over API keys for:
# - Better security (no static credentials)
# - Automatic token rotation
# - Granular permission control
# - Easier credential management
# - Better audit trail

# API keys still supported for:
# - Legacy integrations
# - Development/testing
# - Simple use cases

# Both authentication methods work seamlessly together.
